  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A pressure-collapse technique derived from the Rupture-Song, the orca scarification war-ritual that converts blood-pressure into concussive force. Gnash and Crumble extends that principle outward: the caster channels pressure-depth magic through fractured ice, forcing the water trapped in its crystalline structure to expand in synchronized ruptures. The cascading failures propagate through load-bearing formations, leveling fortifications and scattering defensive lines from within.
    
    The technique was perfected by Ruibung, an exiled orca shaman and follower of Grandfather Blood, during the Frozen Peace. Ruibung deployed it to breach the Aurora Stack's outer defenses during the Mbra∴silentiu, and it served as catalyst for the Thron∴mbra Fracta. Jark Rankzule is also documented as a practitioner.
    
    ## Cost
    
    The physical toll is well-attested: accelerated bone-thinning, chronic cold-blood even in temperate cycles, and progressive vulnerability to the very pressure-depths the practitioner commands. Three of Ruibung's pod-mates died during training, their bodies destroyed beyond recovery. These are not incidental risks. The technique demands the caster unmake the structural integrity of the medium they stand on — a fact that selects, with some efficiency, against the careless.
    
    ## Devotional Practice
    
    Among exiled orca, Gnash and Crumble functions as liturgical sacrament — communion with the dead god whose corpse drifts in the deep. The physical cost is reframed as devotional price: thinning bone and cold blood as offerings rather than tactical liabilities. The distinction between weapon and prayer, in orca practice, has always been thinner than penguin scholars prefer to acknowledge.
